--TEST--
an array element written through a property whose receiver erased — a shape's field, or any property of a plain `object` — is refused where it is written — `rule:types/erased-member-access`, `rule:types/grammar`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/erased-member-access` gave an erased property a name-keyed runtime *fetch* and
// stopped there on purpose. That is enough to read one and not enough to write
// an element through one: `rule:types/arrays` separates the array on the way in, and
// the separated copy needs a slot to land in that a by-name resolution does
// not supply. The plain-`object` half has no element type either — the
// property reads as `mixed`, which is not indexable anywhere else in the
// language — so this is the element-write twin of `E0477`, and the answer is
// the same one: convert the receiver to the class that declares the property.
class Plain {
    public array<string> $rows;

    public function constructor() {
        array<string> $seed = ["a", "b"];
        $this->rows = $seed;
    }
}

array<string> $flat = ["a", "b"];
var $shaped = ({rows: $flat});
$shaped->rows["0"] = "z";

object $erased = new Plain();
$erased->rows["0"] = "z";
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0480]: an array element cannot be written through the erased property `rows`
   --> case.nvs:21:1
%A
error[E0480]: an array element cannot be written through the erased property `rows`
   --> case.nvs:24:1
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
